  "text": "Sure! Below is a Python function that meets the requirements of your task. It:\n\n1. Converts the input string to lowercase (ignoring case).\n2. Removes punctuation (using the `string` module).\n3. Splits the string into words.\n4. Counts the frequency of each word using a dictionary.\n\nHere\u2019s the implementation:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequency of each word\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        if word in histogram:\n            histogram[word] += 1\n        else:\n            histogram[word] = 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))\n# Output: {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))\n# Output: {'python': 2, 'is': 2, 'great': 1, 'fun': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))\n# Output: {'test': 3}\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- The `string.punctuation` includes all standard punctuation marks like `!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{};':\",./<>?` \u2013 you can extend this if you need to support other symbols.\n- The `translate` method is used with a translation table to remove all punctuation at once.\n\nLet me know if you want this to handle more specific punctuation or have case sensitivity requirements!",
